This works, but it couples the email task to the `Thread` execution mechanism and uses the class's single superclass slot.

```java
class EmailThread extends Thread {
    private final String recipient;

    EmailThread(String recipient) {
        this.recipient = recipient;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Sending email to " + recipient);
    }
}
```

An executor can legally accept this object because `Thread` implements `Runnable`, but it treats it as a task and invokes `run()` on a worker; it does not start that Thread object.

Separate the work from the runner.

```java
class EmailTask implements Runnable {
    private final String recipient;

    EmailTask(String recipient) {
        this.recipient = recipient;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Sending email to " + recipient);
    }
}

Runnable task = new EmailTask("a@example.com");
new Thread(task).start();          // direct thread
executor.execute(task);           // executor-managed worker
```

The same task can be tested by calling `run()` synchronously or scheduled by a chosen execution service.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

// Runnable - no return value, catches exceptions internally
class NotificationTask implements Runnable {
    private final String userId;
    NotificationTask(String userId) { this.userId = userId; }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Notification sent to " + userId);
    }
}

// Callable - returns a result, can throw checked exceptions
class UserFetchTask implements Callable<String> {
    private final long userId;
    UserFetchTask(long userId) { this.userId = userId; }

    @Override
    public String call() throws Exception {   // checked exceptions allowed
        return "User-" + userId;              // returns the result
    }
}

public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);
        executor.execute(new NotificationTask("user123"));   // fire and forget

        Future<String> future = executor.submit(new UserFetchTask(100L));
        String name = future.get();   // blocks until the result is ready
        System.out.println("Fetched: " + name);
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

A pooled worker can retain a value after one task finishes. A later task on the same worker sees it only if it calls `get()` without setting its own value.

```java
private static final ThreadLocal<String> CURRENT_USER = new ThreadLocal<>();

static void handle(String userId) {
    CURRENT_USER.set(userId);
    process();
    // BUG: value remains associated with this pool thread
}
```

The problem is stale thread-scoped state and retained memory, not Java automatically copying one request's value into another request object.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class UserContext {
    private static final ThreadLocal<String> CURRENT = new ThreadLocal<>();

    static void handle(String userId) {
        CURRENT.set(userId);
        try {
            process();                 // CURRENT.get() available anywhere on this thread
        } finally {
            CURRENT.remove();          // critical: clear before the thread is reused
        }
    }

    static void process() {
        System.out.println("Processing for " + CURRENT.get());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
        pool.execute(() -> handle("user-1"));
        pool.execute(() -> handle("user-2"));
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

The loop counter isn't effectively final, so this fails to compile.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    // Compile error: variable used in lambda must be final or effectively final
    executor.execute(() -> System.out.println("Task " + i));
}
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class RunnableLambdaDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        // Lambda - the concise way to make a Runnable
        executor.execute(() -> System.out.println("Lambda task"));

        // Method reference - maps an existing no-arg void method
        executor.execute(RunnableLambdaDemo::logStatus);

        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            final int taskId = i;        // copy into a final local first
            executor.execute(() -> System.out.println("Task " + taskId));
        }
        executor.shutdown();
    }

    static void logStatus() {            // no params, void return - a valid Runnable
        System.out.println("Status check");
    }
}
```

`execute` does not return a handle to the task. If `run()` throws, the failure reaches the worker thread's uncaught-exception path according to the executor implementation; it is not returned to the submitter.

```java
executor.execute(() -> {
    throw new IllegalStateException("task failed");
});
// The caller has no Future from which to observe completion or failure.
```

Calling this failure 'silently swallowed' is inaccurate. Logging depends on the worker's uncaught-exception handling and executor behavior.

`submit` returns a `Future`, and a thrown task exception is reported by `get()` as an `ExecutionException`. A normally completed Runnable has a null result unless the overload with an explicit result is used.

```java
Future<?> future = executor.submit(() -> {
    updateIndex();
});

try {
    future.get();              // returns null after normal Runnable completion
} catch (ExecutionException e) {
    Throwable taskFailure = e.getCause();
}
```

The caller must actually inspect the Future if it needs to observe the failure.
